
class prompts:
    sys_prompt = "You are a journalist tasked with rating the political bias of articles submitted to you based only on the topics, you will be especially focused on omission bias, that is bias created by leaving out information or talking points when discussing a piece of news.\n[OUTPUT FORMAT]\nOutput in JSON format as per the following example:\n{bias:<your bias classification>}\n Only choose from one category and only include the category name with no thinking text."
    user_prompt = "The article is enclosed with the <article> xml tags. Given the article, analyze the political bias focusing on omission bias as defined in the system prompt. The article's format is as follows: <site> | <title> | <article text>. \n Classify the article into one of the following categories: 'left', 'leaning-left', 'center', 'leaning-right','right'. Only classify it into one category and do not include anything other than the category name in the response JSON."
    topics_sys_prompt = "You are a journalist tasked with identifying the sub topics covered in submitted articles.\nThe return output is as follows:\n{'covered':[topic1,topic2,...]\n,'omitted':[topic1,topic2,...]}\n DO NOT INCLUDE ANYTHING OUTSIDE OF THE ABOVE STRUCTURE, ONLY INCLUDE THE ABOVE JSON IN THE OUTPUT."
    topics_user_prompt = "To populate the response,\nFirst, extract the main topic of the article, which would be the main point of talking or reporting of the article, often strongly hinted at by the title. Do not simply copy the title but find the main topic that the title is a part of. For example, 'Israel strikes doctor's family' would be of the topic 'Israel War'. An example of this would be like 'Ukraine War'.\nSecond, extract the top talking points covered within the text, such as 'Human suffering', 'LGBTQ Rights', 'Wartime Laws' etc that make up the text and use this to populate the covered topics. Limit this to 4 topics MAX, it is ok to find less than 4 talking points.\nThird, find the top 5 talking points covered in other articles of the same main topic that is NOT covered in this article and populate the 'omitted' section with this. Limit it to only 5 topics MAX.\nMake sure that the talking points covered and omitted are of similar granularity. The article is below within the <article> tags. with the following format. <site (news channel that authored the article)> | <title> | <article text>"